</figure>

Suppose it is not. From the point {D} draw {DE} at right angles to the straight line {AD} in the plane {AB|planeAB}, and draw {DF} at right angles to {CD} in the plane {BC|planeBC}. [!just I.11]

Now, since the plane {AB|planeAB} is at right angles to the plane of reference, and {DE} is at right angles in the plane {AB|planeAB} to {AD}, their intersection, therefore {DE} is at right angles to the plane of reference. [!just XI.Def.4]

Similarly we can prove that {DF} is also at right angles to the plane of reference. Therefore from the same point {D} two straight lines have been set up at right angles to the plane of reference on the same side, which is impossible. [!just XI.13]

Therefore no straight line except the intersection {DB} of the planes {AB|planeAB} and {BC|planeBC} can be set up from the point {D} at right angles to the plane of reference.

Therefore, *if two planes which cut one another are at right angles to any plane, then their intersection is also at right angles to the same plane.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
